[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that settles infrastructure. Now the semantic problem underneath the request.

  In a single-user app, you are the only thing that changes tasks. A notification that says "you changed this task" is
  noise. So "notified when tasks they care about change" needs a real referent, and there are genuinely different ones:

  - [1mTime[0m, not change — due dates arriving, things going overdue, reminders you set. The trigger is the clock, and it
    works fine with one user.
  - [1mChange you didn't make in this moment[0m — you edited something a week ago, or in another tab, or on your phone once
    syncing exists. The trigger is a diff against what you last saw.
  - [1mRules you wrote[0m — "tell me when anything tagged [38;5;153murgent[39m moves to blocked". The trigger is a condition you subscribed
    to, and it's the one that keeps working unchanged when other people arrive.

  These aren't variations on one feature; they're three different engines. Picking more than one is fine, but each is
  real work, and the third is the only one that literally matches "tasks they [3mcare about[0m" — caring is an explicit
  subscription.

  My recommendation: [1mtime-based plus rules[0m, skipping the diff engine. Due dates are what people actually mean by task
  notifications most of the time, and subscription rules are the piece that survives the multi-user transition intact.
  The diff engine is the one that only becomes valuable once there's a second actor — building it now means building it
  for a hypothetical.
